The language can't express this in one query: unbounded traversal is rejected, so `knows{1,}` is refused and any finite bound could miss people on a longer chain. This query gives one step of the closure, and the caller repeats it.

```gq
// Caller: start with $frontier = [from-handle]. Run this query, collect the returned
// (handle, name) rows, and set the next $frontier to the returned handles not seen before.
// Repeat until no new handles appear. The answer is the distinct `name` values over all
// rows collected. The start person is included only if a cycle leads back to them.
query knows_step($frontier: [String]) {
    match { $p: Person, $p knows $f }
    where $p.handle in $frontier
    return distinct { $f.handle, $f.name }
}
```

